    "annotations": [
      {
        "id": "a1",
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "**Belt drive.** Sf Belt imposes the belt speed v_b on the 0-junction Jb, whose effort is the friction force F. The slip w = v_b - x' goes to the friction contact Fric (a nonlinear R, F = F(w)); the rest drives the block's 1-junction Jm: mass m (I), spring k (C), velocity detector Vel and position sensor Pos.",
        "x": 40,
        "y": 680,
        "w": 760,
        "h": 90
      },
      {
        "id": "a2",
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "**Belt-driven Van der Pol oscillator.** This diagram: the friction curve's third-order fit about the belt speed, F = F0 - alpha (w - v_b) + beta (w - v_b)^3, which makes the block obey Rayleigh's equation m x'' - alpha x' + beta x'^3 + k (x - x_s) = 0; its velocity obeys Van der Pol with mu = alpha/sqrt(k m), tau = sqrt(k/m) t, x_vdp = x'/V, V = sqrt(alpha/(3 beta)). Stribeck: the same rig on the full curve. Ref: the textbook equation in the rig's seconds (RefV = V x, in m/s).",
        "x": 40,
        "y": 790,
        "w": 1000,
        "h": 90
      },
      {
        "id": "a3",
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "**Why the viscous term.** alpha = alpha_f - sigma: the Stribeck drop alone (alpha_f = 51.5 N s/m) would grow the velocity swing to the belt speed, and the block would stick (stick-slip, not Van der Pol). The viscous friction sigma = 46.5 N s/m keeps the net slope small: mu = 0.5, peak slip variation 62 % of v_b.",
        "x": 840,
        "y": 680,
        "w": 560,
        "h": 100,
        "color": "blue"
      }
    ]
  },
